n the protocol—an important contextual point for understanding that enrollment does not inherently mean foregoing routine care. [Data: Entities (4264); Relationships (8884)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Myths and misconceptions are a central reputational/informational risk affecting expectations about benefits, risks, and placebo use\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community directly connects CLINICAL TRIAL to MYTHS AND MISCONCEPTIONS, describing widespread incorrect beliefs that distort how people understand trials and shape decisions about participation. This is the strongest “reputation/communication” theme in the data and represents a material risk to informed decision-making rather than a technical or legal noncompliance issue. [Data: Entities (4265); Relationships (8885)].\\n\\nThe misconceptions are described as influencing how people—particularly many people with cancer—evaluate potential benefits and risks, including misunderstandings about harms/uncertainties and placebo use. In practice, this kind of misunderstanding can reduce appropriate enrollment, create unrealistic expectations, or erode trust in research, even when trials are structured with eligibility rules, phased testing, and regulatory oversight. [Data: Entities (4265, 701, 700); Relationships (8885, 1019)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"The community includes disease- and resource-specific references that illustrate how trials connect to real patient populations and information sources\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Several nodes tie clinical trials to specific therapeutic areas and patient populations: therapies designed for primary CNS lymphoma are stated to be studied in clinical trials, and “new therapies” (e.g., for gliomas) are also explicitly described as being studied in trials. This indicates the community is not purely abstract; it is grounded in oncology contexts where investigational options are relevant. [Data: Entities (4159, 5505); Relationships (8630, 12252)]